00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.920
So a conceptual overview of
community visualizations.

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.290
What's actually
happening under the hood

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:48.880
when I click that load button,
when I enter in that ID?

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.190
So when you load a
community visualization,

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.370
the end user is providing
a component ID, which

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.740
is basically saying,
hey, Data Studio,

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.240
this is where to
find the metadata

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.700
about my visualization.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.730
So Data Studio then goes
to read the package data

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:08.240
and it says, OK, this is what
I know about who wrote it.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.250
This is where I can find
the other code and all

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:13.575
the resources that
I need for it.

00:05:13.575 --> 00:05:15.950
Then Data Studio will load
their visualization resources.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.675
So it goes and finds,
OK, you told me

00:05:17.675 --> 00:05:19.550
that this is where I
can find the JavaScript,

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:22.370
the CSS, the configuration.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.070
I'm going to load them in now.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:28.560
And then once it's loaded, the
iframe tells Data Studio, hey,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.980
I'm ready for some data
and styling information.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.910
And then Data Studio provides
that data and style information

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:40.030
so that the code you've
written, the JavaScript

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:42.400
code you've written can
render the visualization.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.680
So transforming that data
and styling information

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.710
that the end user has
provided into a really

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:51.140
cool visualization.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.290
So what are the big steps
involved in building and using

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.010
community visualizations?

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:58.369
So the first is that you
have to write the code.

00:05:58.369 --> 00:05:59.910
So writing the code
involves a couple

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.060
different things, which
I'll walk through in a sec.

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.550
Then you have to upload the
visualization resources.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.740
So right now, we only support
Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:09.904
But broadly, the idea is
put the resources somewhere

00:06:09.904 --> 00:06:12.320
where Data Studio can get to
them so that we can load them

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.970
into your report.

00:06:13.970 --> 00:06:16.520
And then finally, loading the
visualization in Data Studio.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:19.040
What does it look like to take
all the code you've written,

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.210
and see it integrate
with the dashboard?

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.700
So first I'm going to talk
about writing the visualization

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.430
code and the different things
that are associated with that.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:33.160
So there's three steps to
writing a visualization.

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.160
You have to write a
configuration JSON.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:36.870
You have to write the
visualization code,

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.640
and package the visualization.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.680
So we're going to dive
right in and think

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.360
about what the configuration
JSON is, why it's important.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.250
So the configuration
JSON defines two things.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.130
It defines data options
and styling options.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.590
So it's essentially
a JSON with two keys.

00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:58.640
It's a data key, and that
configures the data side

00:06:58.640 --> 00:06:59.840
of the property panel.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:03.241
So it's stuff like, how many
metrics does my visualization

00:07:03.241 --> 00:07:03.740
support?

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.320
How many dimensions does
my visualization support?

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.390
Do I want someone to
only be able to use

00:07:08.390 --> 00:07:10.190
one dimension and one
metric, or should it

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:13.130
be one dimension and between
one and three metrics,

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:16.130
and actually, these metrics
should be named something

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:19.250
really specific to the use
case so that the end user knows

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.170
what kind of metric to put in?

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:23.900
So all of that happens
in the data section.

00:07:23.900 --> 00:07:26.970
And you'll see an
example of that later.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:29.550
With styling, it's
stuff that you

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:33.060
would see on the Style
tab of the property panel.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:36.300
So things like, I want
to name a checkbox.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.190
Should I show labels?

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:40.680
Or I want the end user
to be able to change

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.650
the color of my bar
chart, and I want them

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:46.950
to use a fill picker for that.

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.430
We support a whole bunch
of styling icons that

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.380
allow the end user to
interact with them,

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.380
and then Data Studio will
send whatever they pick back

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:56.940
to your visualization.

00:07:56.940 --> 00:08:00.630
So that's what the
configuration JSON does.

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.740
And this config does two things.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:04.020
One, I just talked about.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:06.550
It impacts how the
property panel renders.

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:09.710
So it impacts how
many dimensions

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:12.970
the end user can put in, how
many metrics they can put in,

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:15.881
what styling options
are available to them.

00:08:15.881 --> 00:08:18.130
But the other thing it does
is it allows the developer

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.260
to know what keys to use
to access the data later.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:26.830
So not only am I saying, provide
me one dimension, but I'll say,

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:28.060
provide me one dimension.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.910
And under the hood, I'm
going to call it dim1 so

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:34.630
that when I go to access it
later in my visualization code,

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:37.870
I'm going to say, I'm going
to use dim1 in my code.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.340
Same with styling.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:45.250
So if I define a color picker
for the color of my text,

00:08:45.250 --> 00:08:47.860
I'll call it maybe text color.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:49.600
And then I can go
back in my code later

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:51.700
and say, give me
the text color thing

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:52.825
that the end user selected.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.880
The next thing is to
write the JavaScript code.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.550
So what goes into the viz code?

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.110
There's three
components that are

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:05.370
necessary for the actual
visualization code itself.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.440
One is the Data
Studio helper library.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:09.000
We've provided a
JavaScript library

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:13.740
that abstracts out some of
the interfacing with our API.

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:15.880
And so that's one piece of it.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:18.210
The second is any JavaScript
visualization library

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.860
you might be
interested in using.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.090
There's a million out there.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:24.545
You all probably
know of D3, Chart.js.

00:09:24.545 --> 00:09:25.920
You might even
have some that are

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.940
internal to your own company.

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:31.070
And finally, there's the
viz code that you write.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:34.530
And so these three combined
go into the viz code.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.360
Optionally, there's
a CSS file where

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:39.180
you can provide additional
styling guidance to the iframe.

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:43.760
And the code that
you would write

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:46.760
within that that's not
the helper library, that's

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.080
not visualization
packages, is going

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.050
to broadly look like this.

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:54.800
The const dscc
require @google/dscc,

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.800
that's just saying that I'm
including the Data Studio

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:01.820
helper library that helped
me write a visualization.

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:03.170
And then I define a callback.

00:10:03.170 --> 00:10:05.420
I'm going to call
it draw chart here.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:08.240
And I'm just going to say,
hey, give me the height.

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:12.410
Give me the width so I know
the dimensions of the canvas

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:14.540
that I get to work with.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:17.180
And then subscribe to the
data so that this draw chart

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:20.300
function is called every time
there's an update to the data

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:23.030
or to the style information
that the end user is selecting

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:25.370
inside the property panel.

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.550
Another required argument to
this subscribe to data function

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:31.280
is a transform just to
understand what data format

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:32.990
would be most useful for you.

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:34.520
And if you go to dev site--

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:37.040
and you'll see that URL later--

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:40.970
the specifics of what
that object looks like

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:44.697
and what information
you get is all there.

00:10:44.697 --> 00:10:47.280
So next, you have to upload the
visualizations to Google Cloud

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.380
Platform.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.570
So you just have a Google
Cloud Platform bucket.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.110
You put your stuff there.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:55.160
And again, broadly, the idea is,
I need to put my code somewhere

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.080
that Data Studio can get to it.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.670
And so right now, it's
Google Cloud Platform.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:03.110
So you put it in.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:05.540
And then using the
location of where you've

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:07.640
put things in Google
Cloud Platform,

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:11.670
loading the visualization
into Data Studio.

