So I've said you can
play with the node color,

00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:56.740
whether or not you show the node
labels, change the font size,

00:12:56.740 --> 00:13:00.190
and the label offset so
that whoever else wants

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:03.040
to use this visualization can
configure all of these things

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:07.060
without ever having to touch
the visualization code.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:10.054
So that's what that looks like.

00:13:10.054 --> 00:13:11.470
Going back to that
tab, you'll see

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.600
it's finally decided to load.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:16.990
So that's how you would load it.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:18.750
So now what I want to do--

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.760
might be a dangerous
proposition when I'm doing

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.240
this on the livestream--

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:25.810
is to show you how I would
build a visualization.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.970
And so generally when
I build visualizations,

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:30.860
I'm starting from something
that already exists.

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:36.910
So yesterday, I took some time
to build a histogram using D3.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.520
So I'm just going to show you
what that looks like locally.

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:44.210
So it's a histogram.

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.130
I have buckets.

00:13:46.130 --> 00:13:48.890
I used the counts of
things in those buckets

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.620
to determine the bar height.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:53.210
But what I'm going
to do now is I

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.540
want to be able to load this
visualization in a Data Studio

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:57.930
report.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:01.690
So to be able to do that, I need
to define a couple of things.

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:03.440
Remember what I was
talking about earlier.

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.840
The first thing to do is to
define the configuration.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:18.390
So with the histogram,
naively, the simplest thing

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.940
I can think of is I want
an index dimension that

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:23.590
just deaggregates my data.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:27.060
And so I just tell Data Studio
I'm going to call it index.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.670
This is the ID that I'm going
to use to access it in my code

00:14:29.670 --> 00:14:31.560
later.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:34.570
I'm going to do this
label thing called index.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:37.770
And this is the label that shows
up in the property panel when

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:39.450
the end user loads
it so they know

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:42.600
they're loading it into
something I'm calling index.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:45.150
It expects that it's
a dimension type.

00:14:45.150 --> 00:14:47.160
And under options,
I'm saying, you

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:50.700
have to have at least one
field populated in here,

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:55.290
and you have to have a maximum
of one field populated in here.

00:14:55.290 --> 00:14:57.870
And then I'm doing the same
thing for a metric and saying,

00:14:57.870 --> 00:15:01.130
this is the thing I'm going to
figure out the distribution of.

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:03.330
And again, I want
only one of these,

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:06.890
so I'm going to
say min max of one.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:09.040
And so this project is
set up with a little bit

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.770
of template code a little
bit of scripting so

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:13.150
that I can just deploy things.

00:15:15.772 --> 00:15:23.152
Run-- And so what
I'm doing here is

00:15:23.152 --> 00:15:24.860
I'm just telling Data
Studio to build it.

00:15:29.230 --> 00:15:34.030
And so once I have
done that, I would

00:15:34.030 --> 00:15:38.140
be able to see those
things show up in the data

00:15:38.140 --> 00:15:40.300
where I would be able to say--

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.030
scrolling up-- that there's
one dimension, and it's called

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:45.370
index, and one metric,
and it's called sessions.

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:53.170
And then the thing I would do in
order to convert the histogram

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:56.500
code from something that was
run locally to something that

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:59.710
is running maybe
in Data Studio is

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:02.900
to add the Data Studio
community component library--

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:04.570
that's the helper library--

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:06.730
and to use that to
interface with the data

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:08.410
instead of maybe
hard coding it, like

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:10.165
I did for the initial demo.

00:16:17.980 --> 00:16:23.140
So this is just the setup
for what I would do here.

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:27.880
And this is where I'm
converting the data

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:30.490
from whatever Data
Studio expects to what

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.260
my visualization expects.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.600
And I'm just saying,
hey, take the data

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.460
from whatever it was
before into what I know

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:38.330
my visualization will take.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.170
I'm going to populate
this drawViz,

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:45.400
and just copy paste all the code
that I had previously into it.

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:48.580
And then this is the
subscribe to data,

00:16:48.580 --> 00:16:51.460
where I'm telling Data Studio,
hey, draw this visualization.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:16:54.400
Run this drawViz
function every time

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.430
I get a new set of data
and styling option.

00:17:05.579 --> 00:17:07.205
So here I'm saying I want D3.

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:21.790
And then for drawViz,
I'm just going

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:26.300
to copy over all of
that code, essentially.

00:17:26.300 --> 00:17:35.070
And instead of height, I'm
going to say, hey, Data Studio,

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:36.660
give me the height
of the iframe,

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:42.302
because the iframe could change,
because a user might decide

00:17:42.302 --> 00:17:44.510
that they want to move things
around in their report,

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:46.080
or resize things.

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.040
And so the height
of the visualization

00:17:49.040 --> 00:17:51.440
isn't necessarily
something you want to fix.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:18:00.477
And dscc-- And then change
the width to do that.

00:18:00.477 --> 00:18:02.310
And then instead of the
data being some kind

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:03.720
of hard coded sample data.

00:18:10.340 --> 00:18:13.010
And so that's really all
it takes to take something

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:16.610
from a visualization that
you've used elsewhere or written

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:23.000
to run locally into something
that you can load into a Data

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:23.750
Studio report.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:30.026
So let's go back here.

00:18:30.026 --> 00:18:30.700
Let's see.

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.670
And I think that's
what I called it.

00:18:44.104 --> 00:18:45.520
And so what that
looks like-- here

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:48.540
you can see that
there's an index,

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:52.280
and that's what supposedly
is deaggregating my data.

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:55.240
This particular dataset
isn't quite set up that way.

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.170
And then a metric.

00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:00.250
And so I can interact
with it, play with it

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:07.320
as if I was interacting
with any other Data Studio

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:08.820
visualization.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:11.520
And then here in
the Style panel,

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:15.520
I have this thing
called a color picker.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:19.914
And if I really wanted
to, I can pick this now.

00:19:19.914 --> 00:19:21.330
I haven't set up
the visualization

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:23.970
to take the styling
information from it.

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:25.680
But then I could go back in.

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.560
And the way I would
do that in the code

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:33.306
is just to figure out where
I set the color of the bar.

00:19:33.306 --> 00:19:36.660
I think it's fill.

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:43.230
And then here, I would just
call it, I think, vizData.style.

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:49.915
And I think I
called it barColor.

00:19:49.915 --> 00:19:51.290
And then that
would be the only--

00:19:51.290 --> 00:19:52.998
it's a one line change
in the code for me

00:19:52.998 --> 00:19:57.900
to be able to take the color
that the end user selects,

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:01.330
and have it show up
in the visualization.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:03.490
So going back.

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:05.890
Just built a visualization.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:09.140
What are some of the
takeaways from this?

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:11.680
One is that the community
visualization, this custom

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:14.050
visualization feature gives
you a blank canvas and data

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:14.950
to play with.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.650
Essentially, we're handing
you an iframe and saying,

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:20.480
what cool things can you
build with your data?

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:25.750
And so you can do things even
with Web Audio, and such,

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:27.490
and WebGL.

00:20:27.490 --> 00:20:30.070
And the other thing is that
the configuration files allow

00:20:30.070 --> 00:20:33.467
you to expose options to the end
user, which means that your end

00:20:33.467 --> 00:20:35.050
user doesn't need
to know how to code,

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:38.170
doesn't need to know D3 or
any other JavaScript chart

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.150
libraries in order to
use what you've built.

00:20:41.150 --> 00:20:43.360
And if you expose
certain options,

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.850
they can have a lot of
freedom and flexibility,

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:48.430
even within that, without
ever having to go back and dig

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:51.440
through the viz code.

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:54.620
So thanks I hope you all
go build a visualization.

00:20:54.620 --> 00:20:57.590
You can find all of the
documentation and tutorials

00:20:57.590 --> 00:20:59.952
on how to do it at
developers.googl

00:20:59.952 --> 00:21:02.199
e.com/datastudio/visualiztion.

00:21:02.199 --> 00:21:03.740
If you build something
cool, share it

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.807
on social media with
the #DataStudioDevs.

00:21:06.807 --> 00:21:08.390
And you can find me
on Twitter @y3l2n.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:14.290
Stay tuned for live Q&amp;A. We will
be back in less than a minute.

00:23:31.250 --> 00:23:32.190
Welcome back.

00:23:32.190 --> 00:23:33.860
So let's answer a
couple questions

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:36.719
about community
visualizations in Data Studio.

00:23:36.719 --> 00:23:39.260
So the first question is, can
I use non-JavaScript libraries,

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:42.800
like Python's matplotlib
or maybe R's ggplot2?

00:23:42.800 --> 00:23:45.180
And the answer to that is no.

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:47.810
We only allow you
to load JavaScript

00:23:47.810 --> 00:23:52.950
within an iframe because of the
nature of how things are setup.

00:23:52.950 --> 00:23:55.770
Next thing is, how can we
only let certain users use

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:56.901
my visualization?

00:23:56.901 --> 00:23:58.650
Right now, we don't
have a way to do that,

00:23:58.650 --> 00:24:02.010
except by limiting who knows
where the resources live

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:05.370
and who knows that
visualization ID.

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:07.890
But that is something we're
looking forward to working on

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:10.650
in the near future.

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:12.390
The next thing is,
what data sources work

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:14.820
with community visualizations?

00:24:14.820 --> 00:24:19.020
And that's basically anything,
as long as the data source

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:21.960
owner allows it, because
we want to make sure

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.780
that people are aware that these
are created by the community

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:25.965
and by third party folks.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:31.620
And for the next thing, where
can you find code examples

00:24:31.620 --> 00:24:33.940
for community visualizations?

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:37.260
If you want to see examples
of community visualizations

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:40.090
or see working examples
from the documentation,

00:24:40.090 --> 00:24:40.800
we have a GitHub.

00:24:40.800 --> 00:24:43.670
So if you go to github.com/googl
edatastudio/comm

00:24:43.670 --> 00:24:48.690
unityvisualizations, you'll
be able to see some examples

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:52.950
of things that are
up on our website.

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:56.640
And then finally, how do
you test the visualization

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.470
as you build it?

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:00.600
And so that's a thing I
was kind of already doing

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.600
is as I was building
it, loading it.

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:07.557
And so a lot of it is
just loading it as you go.

00:25:07.557 --> 00:25:09.140
If you wanted to
maintain separate dev

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:12.750
and prod deployment so that
people who are using your code

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:15.060
aren't broken as you
try to add new features,

00:25:15.060 --> 00:25:19.350
I would just recommend
having two separate locations

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:23.850
of your resources, and just
managing them yourself.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:24.900
That's all for today.

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:27.289
Please tune in next Tuesday
for the next Cloud OnAir,

00:25:27.289 --> 00:25:28.580
and thank you all for watching.

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:31.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

